## API Design

- Validate all input with schema validation in middleware before route handler.
- Use standard error format (RFC 9457 Problem Details or consistent envelope). Use HTTP status codes correctly (400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 422, 429, 500).
- For validation errors: include field-level details in response.
- Early return for errors at function start. Happy path last.
- Never expose stack traces or internal paths in API responses.
- Server-side pagination with enforced max page size.
- Rate limit all public endpoints (not just auth). Include `Retry-After` header on 429 responses.
- Set request timeout on every external HTTP call — AI generates hanging requests by default.
- Never trust client-sent IDs for authorization. Always verify resource ownership server-side (IDOR prevention).

## Auth

- Tokens in httpOnly+Secure+SameSite cookies. Never localStorage.
- Regenerate session ID after login and privilege escalation.
- PKCE for all OAuth authorization code flows.
- Rate limit auth endpoints with progressive delays.
- Access tokens: short-lived (≤15min). Verify issuer, audience, expiry claims.

## Error Handling

- Catch errors as unknown type — narrow before accessing properties.
- Centralized error handler: catches all, maps to response, logs, never leaks internals.
- Handle unhandled exceptions at process level — log and exit gracefully.

## Observability

- Use structured logging (JSON). Never bare debug prints in production.
- Never log PII, credentials, or full request/response bodies.
- Add error tracking service. Health endpoint: `GET /health`.
- Graceful shutdown: on termination signal, stop accepting new requests, drain in-flight, close connections, exit.

## Concurrency

- Idempotency keys on all payment/mutation requests.
- Atomic operations for counters when applicable.
- Auth/payment/mutation changes → require human review.